WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.123
Put the numbers one to a thousand

2
00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.530
into a binary search tree

3
00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:10.140
one after another, in order.

4
00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:12.117
Every new number is bigger

5
00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.300
than all the others

6
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:14.760
so it goes to the right

7
00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:16.277
and to the right again.

8
00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:18.404
The tree turns into a chain

9
00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.290
of a thousand nodes.

10
00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.602
Finding the last number takes

11
00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:24.310
a thousand steps.

12
00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:27.250
The same thousand keys, kept balanced

13
00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:28.160
take ten.

14
00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:31.471
How can a tree keep itself balanced when it

15
00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:34.120
cannot know which keys will arrive next?

16
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.390
That is the job of the AVL tree.

17
00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:40.094
This is the first of four videos

18
00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.450
on balanced search trees.

19
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:44.357
We'll see the rotation

20
00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:46.802
the one move that rearranges a tree

21
00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:48.670
without breaking its order

22
00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:51.346
the AVL rule and its four cases

23
00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:54.032
and why an AVL tree can never

24
00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:56.640
be much taller than log n.

25
00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:59.270
The basic move is a rotation.

26
00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:01.850
Take a node and its right child.

27
00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:03.218
Lift the child up

28
00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:05.180
and let the node become the

29
00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:07.260
child's left child.

30
00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:10.112
The child already had a left subtree.

31
00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:12.150
Its keys sit between the two

32
00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:13.731
nodes in sorted order

33
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:15.883
so that subtree becomes the

34
00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:17.890
node's new right subtree.

35
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:19.770
Nothing else moves.

36
00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:21.703
Read the keys from left to right before

37
00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.590
the rotation and after it

38
00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:25.170
and you get the same sequence.

39
00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.080
Only the heights have changed.

40
00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:29.990
Every node keeps its balance factor

41
00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:32.010
the height of its right subtree

42
00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:34.460
minus the height of its left subtree.

43
00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:37.307
The AVL rule says that this number

44
00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:41.120
is always minus one, zero, or plus one

45
00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:42.930
for every node in the tree.

46
00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.830
Insert a key the ordinary way.

47
00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:48.319
Then walk back up toward the root.

48
00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:51.068
The first node whose factor has become plus

49
00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:53.760
two or minus two is the one to fix.

50
00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:56.750
There are four shapes the trouble can take.

51
00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:59.130
The new key went left of the left child

52
00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:00.730
or right of the right child.

53
00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:02.250
Both are straight lines

54
00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:04.300
and one rotation fixes each.

55
00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:07.420
Left then right, or right then left

56
00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:08.540
are zig-zags.

57
00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:10.358
A single rotation would only

58
00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:11.937
move the zig-zag around

59
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.892
so we first rotate the child

60
00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.860
to straighten it, and then rotate the node.

61
00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:18.930
That is a double rotation.

62
00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.350
Insert thirty, twenty, ten, or ten, twenty

63
00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:26.010
thirty, and one rotation is enough.

64
00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:30.070
Insert thirty, ten, twenty, or ten, thirty

65
00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:31.990
twenty, and it takes two.

66
00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:34.450
All four end up as the same tree

67
00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:37.200
twenty at the top, ten and thirty below.

68
00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:39.390
Let's watch it all run.

69
00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:43.450
Insert one, two, three, in that order.

70
00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:46.630
At three, the root leans two to the right

71
00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:48.980
and one left rotation fixes it.

72
00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:53.880
Insert four, and five.

73
00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:56.480
Five makes the node three lean

74
00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.450
so a second rotation, lower down.

75
00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:02.090
Six makes the root lean again

76
00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:05.290
and a third rotation brings back balance.

77
00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:07.400
Seven needs a fourth.

78
00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:10.580
Seven keys, four rotations

79
00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:13.300
and the tree ends perfectly balanced

80
00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.750
in three levels.

81
00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:18.380
Pause and predict.

82
00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:21.628
A tree holds a million keys

83
00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:23.714
and one insertion makes

84
00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.010
some node lean by two.

85
00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:28.135
How many rotations might it take

86
00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:30.170
to repair the whole tree?

87
00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:31.550
A few thousand?

88
00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:32.880
About twenty?

89
00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:34.620
At most two.

90
00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:37.758
One rotation, or one double rotation.

91
00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.674
After an insertion the tree below the fixed

92
00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.540
node is exactly as tall as before

93
00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:46.190
so everything above it is balanced again.

94
00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:49.800
How tall can an AVL tree get?

95
00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:51.302
Turn the question around

96
00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.782
what is the fewest nodes a tree

97
00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:55.770
of height h can have?

98
00:03:56.120 --> 00:04:00.369
A root, one subtree of height h minus one

99
00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:02.090
and next to it the thinnest

100
00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:03.790
subtree the rule allows

101
00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:05.760
of height h minus two.

102
00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:10.460
So the counts go one, two, four, seven

103
00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:14.780
twelve, twenty, thirty-three, fifty-four

104
00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:16.691
eighty-eight, one hundred forty-three.

105
00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:18.867
Each is one more than the two

106
00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:20.830
before it added together.

107
00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:22.870
That is the Fibonacci pattern

108
00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:24.936
and it grows exponentially.

109
00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:27.917
So the height is at most about one point

110
00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:31.001
four four times log base two of n.

111
00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:32.656
One hundred forty-three nodes

112
00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:34.270
fit in ten levels

113
00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:36.900
where a perfect tree would need eight.

114
00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:39.670
Deleting works the same way.

115
00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:42.515
Remove the key as in any search tree

116
00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:44.571
then rebalance every node

117
00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:45.960
on the way back up.

118
00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:48.500
Take a tree with twenty at the top

119
00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:50.100
ten and thirty below

120
00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.820
and twenty-five and forty under thirty.

121
00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:53.960
Delete ten

122
00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:56.520
and the root leans two to the right.

123
00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:59.030
One left rotation repairs it.

124
00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:00.637
To sum up.

125
00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.043
A rotation changes heights but

126
00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:05.090
never the order of the keys.

127
00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:08.297
The AVL rule keeps every balance factor

128
00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:10.450
between minus one and plus one

129
00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:13.869
and four cases, repaired by one single or

130
00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:15.690
one double rotation

131
00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:17.520
restore it after an insertion.

132
00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:19.419
The price is small.

133
00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:21.876
Inserting all the numbers from one to

134
00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:24.282
one thousand twenty-three in order costs

135
00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:26.900
one thousand thirteen rotations

136
00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:29.510
and leaves a tree of ten levels.

137
00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:31.130
In the code cuts

138
00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:34.140
you'll see all of this in a few dozen lines.

139
00:05:34.767 --> 00:05:37.297
Here is the AVL tree in Java.

140
00:05:37.297 --> 00:05:39.797
We'll read the node, the rotations

141
00:05:39.797 --> 00:05:42.447
the rebalancing step, and then run it.

142
00:05:43.027 --> 00:05:44.389
The tree is generic

143
00:05:44.457 --> 00:05:46.034
keys of any type that can

144
00:05:46.097 --> 00:05:48.737
compare themselves, values of any type.

145
00:05:48.737 --> 00:05:52.077
A node stores a key, a value

146
00:05:52.077 --> 00:05:54.587
two child links, and its height.

147
00:05:54.937 --> 00:05:58.067
A leaf has height one, and a missing child

148
00:05:58.067 --> 00:05:59.617
null, counts as zero.

149
00:05:59.687 --> 00:06:01.663
The balance factor is the right

150
00:06:01.727 --> 00:06:03.660
height minus the left height

151
00:06:03.727 --> 00:06:05.899
and update recomputes a height

152
00:06:05.967 --> 00:06:07.057
from the children.

153
00:06:07.637 --> 00:06:09.967
rotateRight takes y

154
00:06:09.967 --> 00:06:12.587
a node that is too tall on its left.

155
00:06:12.587 --> 00:06:14.477
x is its left child.

156
00:06:14.827 --> 00:06:17.897
y takes over x's right subtree

157
00:06:17.897 --> 00:06:20.627
and x's right link points to y.

158
00:06:20.977 --> 00:06:23.627
We update the heights, y first

159
00:06:23.627 --> 00:06:27.027
and return x, the new top of this subtree.

160
00:06:27.027 --> 00:06:29.447
Each rotation also bumps a counter

161
00:06:29.447 --> 00:06:31.137
so we can count them later.

162
00:06:31.717 --> 00:06:33.780
rebalance recomputes the height

163
00:06:33.847 --> 00:06:35.627
and looks at the factor.

164
00:06:35.627 --> 00:06:38.627
Below minus one, the left side is too tall.

165
00:06:38.627 --> 00:06:41.437
Above plus one, the right.

166
00:06:41.787 --> 00:06:44.217
If the tall child leans the other way

167
00:06:44.217 --> 00:06:45.957
that is the zig-zag.

168
00:06:45.957 --> 00:06:49.177
Rotate the child first, then the node.

169
00:06:49.177 --> 00:06:51.547
Otherwise one rotation is enough.

170
00:06:52.127 --> 00:06:54.957
Keys are compared with compareTo

171
00:06:54.957 --> 00:06:57.457
which returns a negative number, zero

172
00:06:57.457 --> 00:06:58.917
or a positive number.

173
00:06:58.917 --> 00:07:01.317
Smaller goes left, bigger goes right

174
00:07:01.317 --> 00:07:03.647
and equal replaces the value.

175
00:07:03.997 --> 00:07:05.884
The recursive call returns the

176
00:07:05.947 --> 00:07:08.524
new top of the subtree, we store it back

177
00:07:08.587 --> 00:07:10.634
and on the way up every node

178
00:07:10.707 --> 00:07:12.357
goes through rebalance.

179
00:07:12.937 --> 00:07:15.547
Insert one to seven in order.

180
00:07:15.547 --> 00:07:18.267
Three triggers the first rotation

181
00:07:18.267 --> 00:07:21.747
five the second, six the third

182
00:07:21.747 --> 00:07:23.417
seven the fourth.

183
00:07:23.767 --> 00:07:26.597
The tree ends as four on top

184
00:07:26.597 --> 00:07:28.637
with two and six below.

185
00:07:28.637 --> 00:07:31.437
And a thousand twenty-three keys, in order

186
00:07:31.437 --> 00:07:34.037
make a tree of ten levels

187
00:07:34.037 --> 00:07:36.967
after one thousand thirteen rotations.

188
00:07:37.317 --> 00:07:39.567
The output is identical

189
00:07:39.567 --> 00:07:41.087
character for character

190
00:07:41.087 --> 00:07:42.307
to the Python program.

191
00:07:42.307 --> 00:07:44.557
Same algorithm, same tree.

192
00:07:45.137 --> 00:07:46.328
Does it really work?

193
00:07:46.387 --> 00:07:48.679
The JUnit test inserts and

194
00:07:48.767 --> 00:07:50.615
deletes random keys

195
00:07:50.707 --> 00:07:52.790
three hundred operations on

196
00:07:52.867 --> 00:07:54.607
each of thirty trees

197
00:07:54.607 --> 00:07:57.897
with the JDK's TreeMap as the reference.

198
00:07:58.247 --> 00:07:59.777
After every operation

199
00:07:59.777 --> 00:08:01.477
it calls checkInvariants

200
00:08:01.477 --> 00:08:03.887
and compares the keys with the reference.

201
00:08:04.237 --> 00:08:07.667
If a rotation ever breaks a link, a height

202
00:08:07.667 --> 00:08:09.287
or the key order

203
00:08:09.287 --> 00:08:12.197
the test fails at the first bad step.

204
00:08:12.777 --> 00:08:14.575
Watch out for three bugs.

205
00:08:14.647 --> 00:08:16.155
Forgetting to store the value

206
00:08:16.207 --> 00:08:17.747
the recursive call returns

207
00:08:17.747 --> 00:08:20.977
so the parent still points to the old node.

208
00:08:21.327 --> 00:08:23.289
Updating heights in the wrong

209
00:08:23.357 --> 00:08:25.157
order after a rotation.

210
00:08:25.157 --> 00:08:28.807
And comparing keys with double equals.

211
00:08:29.157 --> 00:08:31.368
For boxed integers above one

212
00:08:31.447 --> 00:08:32.807
hundred twenty-seven

213
00:08:32.807 --> 00:08:34.907
double equals compares references

214
00:08:34.907 --> 00:08:35.847
not values.

215
00:08:35.847 --> 00:08:37.357
Always use compareTo.

216
00:08:37.707 --> 00:08:40.216
And test after every operation.

217
00:08:40.297 --> 00:08:43.426
Our checkInvariants throws an AssertionError

218
00:08:43.497 --> 00:08:46.297
the moment the key order, a stored height

219
00:08:46.297 --> 00:08:48.627
or a balance factor is wrong.

220
00:08:49.207 --> 00:08:50.817
In the standard library

221
00:08:50.817 --> 00:08:53.477
Java's TreeMap is a balanced search tree

222
00:08:53.477 --> 00:08:55.797
but a red-black tree, not an AVL tree.

223
00:08:55.797 --> 00:08:57.927
We'll build that in video three.

224
00:08:58.277 --> 00:09:01.247
Next time, a different way to stay balanced

225
00:09:01.247 --> 00:09:03.917
nodes that hold more than one key.

